WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:05.040
- The idea that there is
an inevitable trajectory

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.290
towards complex life and perhaps towards

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.833
something like humans is a
little bit wishful thinking.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:15.363
The origin of complex cells
happened just once on earth.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.750
If we think about other planets,

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.243
the likelihood is we'll find bacteria.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:26.040
And this idea that we
are somehow more evolved

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.713
than bacteria is not really true.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:32.423
I work on the deep history of life--

00:00:32.423 --> 00:00:33.256
the origin of life

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.200
through to the evolution of early cells

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.450
and what that means for us today.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.620
Why do we age?

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:41.880
Why do we get diseases?

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.140
Why are we conscious?

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.320
Why have there been long gaps,
two or three billion years

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.240
where it's just bacteria and nothing else?

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.740
And you can't explain that
just in terms of genes.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.540
We wouldn't know anything
about what happened

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.460
in the first two billion
years of life on Earth

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.800
if it hadn't been for gene sequencing.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.780
But life is not only about information.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.370
It's about the structure of cells.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.530
It's about the turnover of molecules,

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.360
how gases in the environment

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.340
are converted into more molecules.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.810
None of this has anything
to do with genes.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.583
All of this happens spontaneously.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:16.110
I think my work's important
because it's asking

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:19.050
some of the biggest questions in science.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.940
What I'm doing is putting back a side

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.710
that's been missing for a long time,

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.780
which is energy flow and its importance,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.743
and how that changes the
way that evolution happens.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.490
The strange thing is very
few people in the world

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:34.110
are thinking along those lines,

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.320
and it has an ability to
come up with an almost

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.813
alternative view of the
whole history of life.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:42.690
If I say "origin of life" to you,

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.630
virtually everybody will
say "primordial soup."

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.854
I think the idea of the primordial
soup is a terrible idea.

00:01:48.854 --> 00:01:50.670
It just won't go away.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.890
The thing about life is it has structure.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:53.820
It has movement.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.710
It is a continuous chemical reaction,

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.420
and the problem with any kind of soup

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.550
is it's basically not reactive.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.040
It doesn't have the flow
of energy going through it.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.380
And so I think it's a really bad way

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.430
of seeing the origin of life,

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:06.577
and I much prefer to think about,

00:02:06.577 --> 00:02:07.800
"What are cells doing?"

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.690
And "What's the simplest version

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.330
of how cells become animated?"

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.880
"Are there geological
environments that work that way?"

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.860
And the answer is yes, there are.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.650
I think life started
down in the deep oceans

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.420
in hydrothermal vents,

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.810
but the earth was very
different four billion years ago

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.540
because there was no oxygen back then.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.540
And if you're working on the
origin of life, which I do,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.020
then you're starting with really
simple prebiotic chemistry.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:37.530
We're trying to do it under
strictly anoxic conditions.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.840
So in the strict absence of oxygen.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.240
But the questions are
really very simple questions

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.880
about how can we have the
simplest possible version

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.920
of a hydrothermal vent in a lab?

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.110
So we use, in a sense,
as little technology

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.310
as we can get away with.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.350
Nobody can agree about what
complex life actually means.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.930
What I mean is the
difference between bacteria

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.860
and the kind of cells that
make up animals and plants,

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:06.027
and single-celled organisms
that eat other things.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.370
Our kind of cell, it's
called the eukaryotic cell

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:12.780
which just means it's got a nucleus

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.510
and the DNA's packaged
away in the nucleus.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:18.603
They're a lot bigger, a lot
more complex than bacteria.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.640
All these complex cells in
plants, in animals, in fungi--

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.893
they all have basically
the same structure.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:28.410
Our kind of cell arose

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.590
once in four billion years of evolution.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.510
And it seems to have been something

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.927
of a bit of a freak accident.

00:03:37.811 --> 00:03:40.170
For two billion years
we have just bacteria,

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:43.260
and the bacteria remain as bacteria.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:44.940
Then we have an abrupt single origin

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.920
of all complex cells that happens

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.050
around two billion years ago.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.630
And then we have another
long period of stasis

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.580
until the Cambrian explosion
where suddenly animals appear.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:57.600
Nearly a hundred million years after that,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.100
we finally get plants on land.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.550
Hundreds of millions of years longer,

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.830
and then we are beginning
to see humans evolving,

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:07.860
and consciousness, as
we would recognize it.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.170
What explains these long gaps?

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:13.410
What kind of evolutionary
process gives rise

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:17.823
to two types of life that live
in completely different ways?

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.330
Perhaps it's nothing to do
with genes and information.

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:24.810
Perhaps it's something to do
with the cell's structure.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:28.560
So we have these mitochondria,
which were bacteria once,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.473
and they seem to have arisen
around two billion years ago.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.530
And the answer is almost certainly,

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.903
that another cell got inside,

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:40.020
and that changes the topology.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.720
It changes the way that
energy works in cells.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.600
Now it's internalized, and
that means you can expand.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.460
You can do different things.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:50.793
It opens up the floodgates to evolution.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.220
We are a continuous chemical reaction

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.220
and we are continually
burning food and oxygen.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:09.900
Life has a very central logic,
which is to do with growth.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.250
Growth is really just doubling.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:12.990
It's converting whatever's
in the environment

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:14.223
into more of yourself.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:17.340
And we've got a circulatory system

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.290
which is continually delivering oxygen

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.320
right to the mitochondria
deep within cells.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:23.940
Cells are tiny things

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.250
and the membrane is
almost invisibly small.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:29.040
If you were to iron out the
membranes in the mitochondria

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:30.150
in our own body,

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:32.790
you'd get about four football
pitches' worth of membranes,

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:35.820
and all those membranes
have got a charge across it.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.110
Which, if you were to shrink yourself down

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:38.880
to the size of a molecule,

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.070
is equivalent to a bolt of lightning.

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:43.140
That's what's animating us.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.390
We're basically powered by electricity.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:50.307
We would not be moving at all
if it wasn't for energy flow.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:54.240
That gives you different insights.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.760
Genes work in a specific environment.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:58.470
That environment is cells.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.390
Cells have a particular structure

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:01.740
and that structure conditions

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.660
what the genes can and cannot do,

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:05.100
and so when we bring energy into it,

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:06.120
we begin to understand

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.620
this peculiar trajectory of life on earth.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:15.930
See, one thing in biochemistry
which is really striking,

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:17.790
the way that plant cells work,

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:19.770
the way that bacteria work,

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:22.260
the way that animals work,

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:23.910
it's all the same.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:27.180
There is a commonality
that gives me a very

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:30.510
strong fellow feeling
with the rest of life.

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:33.723
This deep similarity in everything.

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:36.933
Then you have this amazing variation.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:40.350
It's astonishing that you can use

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:42.510
these building blocks
to come up with a world

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:44.430
which is so different.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:47.323
That is the glory of evolution.

